+| Interface (ImGui) | Written, unrun |
+| Camera detector in the draw path, SEH-guarded read | Written, unrun |
+| HUD-less capture | Written, unrun |
+| Video memory reader (DXGI) | Written, unrun |
+| Draw observation feeding the segmenter | Written, unrun |
+| Installer and uninstaller | Written, unrun |
+| Crash guard and subsystem switches | Written, unrun |
+| Diagnostic report on the clipboard | Written, unrun |
+
+The compute shaders are now compiled by `tools/build.ps1` with `/WX`, so a
+shader that does not compile fails the build rather than becoming a pass that
+silently does nothing. That check verifies syntax and bindings. It cannot verify
+that the arithmetic still matches the C++ it was transcribed from.
+
+---
+
+## Not built
+
+These are the things between here and "everything", with what each actually
+requires. None of them are small, and none of them can be written responsibly
+without a machine to run them on.
+
+### 1. The compute scaffold
+
+Pipeline layouts, compute pipelines, descriptor binding, dispatch, and the
+resource lifetimes around them. The overlay mask needs it, the composite needs
+it, and the native backend needs the same one.
+
+**Why it is not written blind.** The syntax check has been worth having because
+the stubs mirror an API that was understood correctly. For the dispatch path
+that is not true — writing both the code and the stub from the same uncertain
+memory produces a check that validates a guess against itself. That is worse
+than no check, because it reads like verification. This needs the real headers
+in front of it.
+
+**Needs:** Windows, the ReShade SDK, one run.
+
+### 2. The calibration screen — built
+
+A Materials tab over `needs_calibration()`, appearing only when there is
+something to answer, and the provider's side of the exchange: it writes what it
+has seen every few seconds and takes back whatever was answered.
+
+One honest gap, recorded where it matters rather than buried: ReShade's draw
+event does not carry the blend state, and blending is what separates a
+transparent surface from an opaque one. So the provider passes `blended` as
+false and the Transparent class is never derived — glass and foliage are simply
+not offered for calibration yet, rather than being offered on the strength of a
+signal we do not have. Subjects are unaffected; they are found by depth and
+presence, both of which are observed.
+
+**Still unrun**, like the rest of the Windows layer.
+
+### 3. Jitter and the game's TAA
+
+Driving the projection offset per frame, and turning off the game's own temporal
+antialiasing. Without both, render scale 1.00 gives no antialiasing benefit and
+anything below 1.00 is not reconstruction, it is a blur.
+
+**Needs:** the camera constant buffer to be writable, not just readable. The
+detector already finds it; writing to it is a different risk class.
+
+### 4. The native backend, first form
+
+NGX directly, one feature handle, the image pass only, at render scale 1.00.
+This is what turns `backend.hpp` from an interface into a choice.
+
+**Needs:** the NVIDIA NGX SDK, an RTX card, and the compute scaffold.
+
+### 5. The second feature handle
+
+Makes the two-pass dial and the per-material control mask real rather than
+composited. Small once (4) exists — it is one more handle.
+
+### 6. D3D11 ↔ D3D12 interop, DLSS-G, Reflex
+
+The largest single piece, and deliberately last because it can destabilise
+everything before it. Frame generation is D3D12-only; the game is D3D11.
+
+**Needs:** (4), plus a repro harness, plus real measurement. Reflex is not
+optional alongside generation — without it the held frame lands on an already
+queued present and the input lag roughly doubles.
+
+### 7. Per-object motion vectors
+
+Shader injection, so that things moving under their own power — traffic,
+pedestrians — are described correctly rather than by the camera layer. Until
+this exists, `MayDriveFrameGeneration()` is false for every source the system
+can obtain, and that is enforced in code rather than remembered.
+
+---
+
+## The honest summary
+
+The half of this product where mistakes are invisible is finished and checked.
+The half where mistakes are visible is written and unrun, and the largest pieces
+of it are not written at all.
+
+Two things follow, and they are the actual answer to "is it done":
+
+**Nothing here will be finished by more writing.** Every remaining item is
+gated on a machine — Windows, an RTX card, the SDKs, and the game. The next real
+progress is a first run, not a next file. A first run will find things, and
+finding them is the point.
+
+**Nothing claims otherwise in the meantime.** Every unbuilt piece is a
+capability that cannot reach ACTIVE, the interface says which of them is
+missing and why, and the product with all of it switched off behaves exactly as
+it did before any of it was written. That property has been maintained
+deliberately at every step, and it is what makes shipping an unfinished thing
+honest rather than reckless.
+
+The version number goes up when something is built. It does not go up when
+something is claimed.
+
+---
+
+## A review pass before the first run
+
+Written code that has never executed gets one thing that running code gets for
+free: someone reading it again, on purpose, looking for the failure rather than
+for the feature. This is what that found.
+
+**A texture leaked when the device changed.** `HudlessCapture::release` took the
+caller's device and skipped the free when it did not match the one that had
+created the texture — which is precisely the case it existed to handle, so a
+resolution change on a second device leaked. It now frees through the owner
+always, and the device-destroyed path is what covers an owner that is gone.
+
+**A comment could parse as an assignment.** The calibration file's reader
+dropped the `#` token and then read the words after it, so `# 0000000000002222
+skin` in a comment was an assignment. The file this product writes is six lines
+of explanation followed by data, so that is not a corner case. A comment is now
+the whole line from the first `#`, with a test that the written file does not
+parse its own header as data.
+
+**The catalogue re-derived every frame.** Walking a few thousand shaders on the
+present thread, at frame rate, to reach an answer that moves over minutes. That
+is the kind of cost that never shows up in a profile as one expensive thing and
+does show up as a frame time slightly worse than it was. Throttled to once a
+second, which is still more often than the input can change.
+
+**The capture was gated on the wrong thing.** It compared a draw index against a
+record that could be switched off underneath it. It happened to be safe — the
+arithmetic could not collide — but relying on that is not the same as saying it,
+so it is now gated on the record as well as on itself.
+
+**Four headers arrived transitively.** `<cctype>`, `<cstdlib>`, `<string>`,
+`<string_view>` were reached through other includes on the check toolchain.
+That is the classic way code compiles on one compiler and not the next, and
+MSVC is the next one.
+
+None of these would have been found by the type checker, and three of them would
+have been found by the first run in the worst possible way: as a memory leak
+nobody could explain, a calibration that silently applied the wrong thing, and a
+frame time that got slightly worse for no visible reason.
diff --git a/v3/docs/FIRST-RUN.md b/v3/docs/FIRST-RUN.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..eb6d946
--- /dev/null
+++ b/v3/docs/FIRST-RUN.md
@@ -0,0 +1,135 @@
+# The first run
+
+The portable decision layer is heavily tested and the Windows add-ons are built in CI, but a real FiveM/GPU session is still the validation that matters. This document turns a player test from "it didn't work" into useful evidence.
+
+Read it before starting the game, not after.
+
+## Before anything
+
+Copy the whole folder somewhere you can delete. Nothing here modifies the game's
+own files, and `UNINSTALL.bat` replays the install journal backwards — but a
+first run is a first run.
+
+Use only the successful GitHub Actions player artifact. CI runs the 298 core checks, type-checks the Windows layer, compiles every compute shader with `/WX`, builds both add-ons with MSVC, verifies export markers, and exercises the installer/uninstaller against a disposable FiveM layout.
+
+## What should happen, in order
+
+Each of these is a separate mechanism that can fail on its own. Check them in
+this order, because each depends on the ones above it.
+
+**1. The overlay appears.** ReShade's menu has a SECRET EMKO page. If not, the
+add-on did not load: check ReShade's own log for the add-on name.
+
+**2. The header shows two badges.** IMAGE and FRAMES. Both showing something
+other than ACTIVE is correct on a first run.
+
+**3. The Status page lists capabilities with reasons.** Every line that is not
+ACTIVE should say *why* in a sentence. A line with no reason is a bug in this
+product, not in your setup — that is exactly what the evidence model exists to
+prevent, and it is worth reporting.
+
+**4. Frames still arrive.** The base-rate limiter and the draw observation both
+run in the render path. If the game is slower than it was with nothing
+installed, stop here and say so — that is the most important single finding a
+first run can produce.
+
+**5. The camera locks.** Advanced page, after a minute of driving around. It
+searches by behaviour and reports `searching` → `settling` → `locked`. Locking
+on a menu screen and never letting go would be a bug; losing the lock when a
+cutscene starts is correct.
+
+**6. Frame segmentation locks.** Also a minute or so. `FrameSegmentation`
+reaching READY means the system found where the interface starts. It needs the
+frame to hold still for 30 frames, and drops it after 6 that disagree.
+
+**7. The HUD-less copy runs.** `HUD-less image` reaching ACTIVE means a texture
+copy is happening every frame, immediately before the first interface draw. If
+it goes to ACTIVE and back repeatedly, the boundary is not stable — worth
+reporting with a note about what was on screen.
+
+**8. A Materials tab appears.** Only once there is something to answer. It lists
+the busiest draws it could not identify, most-drawn first.
+
+## What to expect to be off
+
+These are not faults. Each is a capability with no evidence behind it yet:
+
+- Frame generation cannot reach ACTIVE. Per-object motion vectors do not exist,
+  and the arbitration layer refuses generation without them, in code.
+- The two-pass dial does nothing. No provider here exposes two feature instances.
+- The overlay mask and composite do not run. Their models are written and tested;
+  nothing dispatches them yet.
+- Render scale below 1.00 is not reconstruction. Jitter is not driven.
+
+If any of these *does* claim to work, that is the serious kind of bug — the
+product's core promise is that a status word means what it says.
+
+## If it crashes, it handles the next start itself
+
+A marker file is written when the add-on starts and removed when it shuts down
+cleanly. Finding one at the next startup means the previous session never got
+that far.
+
+The response is graded, because one unclean exit is weak evidence — games crash
+on their own, and so do drivers:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **One** | The scene copy stands down. It is the only mechanism that allocates a texture and writes into the live frame. Everything else keeps running. |
+| **Two in a row** | Everything experimental is off and the picture is exactly what the game renders. |
+
+Both say so on screen, with what clears them. The counter resets once a session
+has run ninety seconds, so a crash after five minutes counts as the first rather
+than the next — one bad afternoon cannot leave you permanently in safe mode.
+
+The base-rate cap is never taken away by this. It is not experimental, and
+removing it would change how the game feels for a reason that has nothing to do
+with it.
+
+`SecretEMKO.running` beside the add-on is the marker. Deleting it by hand is a
+supported way out.
+
+## Narrowing it yourself
+
+The Advanced page lists every mechanism that runs inside the render path, each
+with a switch and a line saying what turning it off costs. Switching one off and
+trying again is worth far more than a report that says it crashed — it turns
+eight suspects into one.
+
+The guard only ever narrows. Something you switched off stays off through a
+clean startup; it will not be quietly switched back on.
+
+## If it crashes
+
+Three places are most likely, in this order, and each names itself in the log:
+
+**The camera detector**, reading a constant buffer at unmap. It copies through
+a structured-exception guard before anything downstream sees the bytes, so a
+crash here would mean the guard is not covering what it should.
+
+**The HUD-less capture**, the only code that allocates a texture and issues a
+copy. It disables itself for the session on the first failure rather than
+retrying, and logs the reason with the prefix `SECRET EMKO FG:`.
+
+**The draw observation**, which runs on every draw call. It allocates nothing
+per draw and caps at 8192 draws per frame.
+
+`SecretEMKO.log` sits beside the add-on and carries every status transition with
+a timestamp. ReShade's own log carries anything logged before ours opened.
+
+## What is worth sending back
+
+In rough order of value:
+
+1. **A performance regression.** Numbers before and after, same scene.
+2. **A status line with no reason**, or a status word that overstates what is
+   happening.
+3. **A crash**, with `SecretEMKO.log` and ReShade's log.
+4. **`SecretEMKO-Materials.observed.txt`** after ten minutes of ordinary play.
+   That file is the raw material for a shipped catalogue, and one person's is
+   enough for everyone on the same game build.
